WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.798
In the classic visual question answering task,

00:00:02.878 --> 00:00:06.551
a model takes an image and a question and returns
an answer.

00:00:06.631 --> 00:00:10.242
The field has poured enormous effort into raising
accuracy,

00:00:10.322 --> 00:00:13.433
thanks to large pre-trained vision-language models,

00:00:13.513 --> 00:00:18.125
but almost no attention is paid to how a model
actually reaches its answer.

00:00:18.205 --> 00:00:21.565
The result is a black box: the prediction may be right,

00:00:21.645 --> 00:00:25.319
yet there is no evidence explaining the reasoning
behind it.

00:00:25.399 --> 00:00:30.198
The authors distinguish three kinds of models:
those that give only an answer,

00:00:30.278 --> 00:00:33.076
those that add a generic caption of the image,

00:00:33.156 --> 00:00:39.144
and the rare third kind that produces a logically
self-contained explanation matching the answer.

00:00:39.224 --> 00:00:43.773
Almost all state-of-the-art VQA models fall into
the first two categories,

00:00:43.853 --> 00:00:45.400
which motivates this work.
